## Tips

- **Detail stories before planning** — create tasks for all stories before phase planning, so the plan shows real scope
- **AC must be verifiable** — "works" is bad, "test passes" is good
- **Update progress immediately** — don't defer
- **Use checklist** for tracking subtasks within a task
- **Bugs live in stories** — always attach to a relevant story
- **Sub-agents write tests** — every sub-agent is responsible for tests in its scope
- **Supervisor coordinates reviews** — coordinator must spawn/review-route child reviewer work before marking done
- **Orchestrator closes the loop** — the parent owns producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer until accepted `done`; it never stops at `to-review` or asks the human to perform routine review
- **Visualize at milestones** — render graphs before, during, and after execution with `task-board plan --render`
- **Verify agents early** — run `task-board q 'agents()'` within 2 minutes of launch; if status not updated, stop and restart with better prompt
- **Concrete IDs only** — never use placeholders like `TASK-XXX` in prompts; always use real IDs like `TASK-260203-ae3fcd`
- **Human-facing references need ID + title/name** — when mentioning an epic, story, task, or bug in chat, include both so people can understand the reference without looking up the ID
- **No work without a task** — every code change must be tracked on the board. If a task doesn't exist, create one first. Don't start coding then retroactively create tasks
- **Parent is orchestrator, not worker** — the parent agent plans, creates/refines board items, spawns children, watches `RUN-...` handles, sends directives, and decides next routing. It must not do implementation, research, testing, docs, or review inline when a board task can be delegated.
- **Do not force-fit broken assumptions** — pause only for a concrete external blocker or an evidence-backed unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision. Document the evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact decision or external input needed; do not build a tower of stubs, flags, priority rules, and tests around an invalid model.
- **All task work through tracked background spawn** — every board task MUST run in a separate CLI process with explicit `--role`, `--background`, `--model`, and Codex `--reasoning-effort`. Never use an untracked Task helper or implicit model/reasoning selection for board deliverables.
- **Reviews are child work too** — when work reaches `to-review`, spawn an explicitly selected reviewer child such as `task-board spawn TASK-... --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`. Route accepted work to `done` or requested changes into rework and another reviewer cycle.
- **`to-review` is not done** — producers must report `to-review` as "ready for review" or "handed off to review". Coordinators must not tell the human "done"/"complete"/"finished"/"final"/"готово" until a reviewer has accepted the work and the board item is actually `done`.
- **Coordinators track `RUN-...`, not shell jobs** — after spawning, hold onto the returned run id and drive orchestration with `task-board spawn status/events/watch/wait`. Do not rely on `&`, `nohup`, or runtime background wrappers to track completion
- **Drive unblocked tasks to terminal handoff** — if no objective blocker remains, the spawned worker must keep going until it has checked the relevant checklist items, attached outcome evidence, and reached its producer `end_status` (`to-review`) or exactly one reviewer verdict status (`done`, `to-dev`, `analysis`, or evidence-backed `blocked`). If a child exits early or leaves a task parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing`, retry or reroute through a focused child. Use `blocked` only for an evidence-backed external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision, with the full evidence packet and exact human decision or external input needed to resume.
- **Dependent children are sequenced** — if child B consumes or reviews child A's output, the parent must `spawn wait` child A, inspect status/outcome resources, and only then spawn child B. Parallel fan-out is only for independent tasks.
- **Explicit spawn selection is REQUIRED** — always pass `--role`, `--background`, and `--model`; Codex also needs a compatible `--reasoning-effort`. The role drives identity/lifecycle, while model and reasoning are deliberate caller choices
- **`project_config()` first** — before spawn or spawn-contract review, run `task-board q 'project_config()'` so you know whether the current cwd is local or remote, whether `spawn.enabled` permits launch, and which model criterion is in effect
- **Resource CRUD is explicit** — `add_resource` / `resource add` create a new named resource and must not silently overwrite; use `update_resource` / `resource update` to replace an existing resource; use `delete_resource` / `resource delete` to remove one
- **Inline text resources via DSL** — use `task-board m 'add_resource(...)'` for create and `task-board m 'update_resource(...)'` for replacement when the payload is short text that already fits naturally in DSL
- **File and URL attachments via CLI** — use `task-board resource add TASK-... ./file.png --type precondition`, `task-board resource update TASK-... ./file.png`, and `task-board resource delete TASK-... NAME`
- **Artifact names must include the task ID** — when attaching outcome artifacts or task-specific instructions, include the owning task ID in the resource name, e.g. `TASK-260218-abc123_results.md`
- **Chat attachments need the bridge, not the board** — if the file exists only as a chat attachment and has no local path, install [`alexis-agents-infra`](https://github.com/relux-works/alexis-agents-infra), run `agents-attachments materialize`, then pass the resolved path into `task-board resource add`
- **Precondition resources = agent instructions** — when spawning via `task-board spawn`, precondition resources are auto-injected into the agent prompt via the template
- **Ignore stale `.temp` worktrees during audits** — when reviewing help/docs/source for spawn drift, search canonical source paths with `.temp/**` excluded; historical `.temp/*/skill-project-management-worktree/` copies can contain obsolete spawn files
- **Research goes to `.research/` AND resources** — all research results persist in `.research/{YYMMDD}_{topic}.md` (project-level) AND as outcome resources on the task. See [`references/research-workflow.md`](references/research-workflow.md)
- **Plans go to `.planning/`** — after phase planning, persist them with `task-board plan --save` to `.planning/{YYMMDD}_{HHmmss}_{topic}.md` for session recovery. See [`references/planning-workflow.md`](references/planning-workflow.md)
- **Legacy token in config** — if you see `remote.token` in `task-board.config.json`, suggest running `scripts/migrate-credentials.sh --user USER_ID` to move it to the credentials store

---

## Remote Board Mode

The board can run as a centralized web service instead of local `.task-board/` directory. Multiple agents connect to the same board via HTTP.

### Setup

```bash
# Start board-server
cd ~/src/skill-project-management/tools/board-server
go run . --data-dir /tmp/board-server-data/boards --auth-db /tmp/board-server-data/auth.db --addr :8090

# Set environment (token from first-run output)
export TASK_BOARD_REMOTE=http://localhost:8090
export TASK_BOARD_TOKEN=tb_xxxxx
export TASK_BOARD_ID=my-project

# Create a board
curl -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $TASK_BOARD_TOKEN" \
  -d '{"id":"my-project","name":"My Project"}' \
  $TASK_BOARD_REMOTE/admin/boards

# Use normally — q and m route to server automatically
task-board q --format compact 'summary()'
task-board m 'create(type=epic, name=my-feature)'
```

### How It Works

- `task-board q` and `task-board m` detect `TASK_BOARD_REMOTE` env and route DSL to the server via HTTP
- `task-board q 'schema()'` and `task-board q 'project_config()'` are resolved locally so agents can inspect the current repo runtime even when normal reads/writes target a remote board
- All mutations (status changes, linking, assignment, auto-promotion) happen server-side
- Multiple agents can work on the same board concurrently — bbolt handles serialization
- `task-board resource add/update/delete/get` work in both local and remote mode for normal local-path and URL attachments
- [`alexis-agents-infra`](https://github.com/relux-works/alexis-agents-infra) is only needed when the file exists only as a chat attachment and must be materialized first
- `grep`, `validate`, `plan --render`, `tree` are local-only (not available in remote mode)
- `spawn` reads board info via remote, but launches agent process locally
- `task-board export` / `task-board import` work in both local and remote mode

### Config Resolution

Priority:
- explicit remote: `--remote` flag > `TASK_BOARD_REMOTE` env
- mode selection: `TASK_BOARD_MODE` env > `task-board.config.json` (`mode`) > default `local`
- unified config path override: `TASK_BOARD_CONFIG` (otherwise `./task-board.config.json`)
- mode=`remote`: use `remote` section (`url`, `board`, `tls_no_verify`) — **no token or active user in config file**
- mode=`local`: use `local` section (`board_dir`, relative path)
- `task-board q 'project_config()'` is the supported preflight check for agents; it shows configured mode, effective mode after explicit overrides, board source, whoami, `spawn.enabled`, and per-agent model policy for the current cwd

Token resolution (in order):
1. `TASK_BOARD_TOKEN` env
2. macOS Keychain (`remote.url + selected user`)
3. Error: no token — run `task-board login USER TOKEN` or set `TASK_BOARD_TOKEN`

Keychain-first auth model:
- project config pins only shared board context: `remote.url`, `remote.board`, `tls_no_verify`
- selected user lives in non-versioned local credentials state scoped by `remote.url + remote.board` (and legacy `remote.user` is only a backward-compat fallback)
- token lives in Keychain, not in git and not in `task-board.config.json`
- `TASK_BOARD_TOKEN` is env override/bootstrap only, not the normal storage path
- `task-board login/logout/switch-user/whoami` manage the selected-user flow

Global non-versioned auth state file:
- path: `~/.config/task-board/credentials.json`
- shape:

```json
{
  "remotes": {
    "https://board.example.com": {
      "active_user": "admin",
      "active_users": {
        "my-project": "alexis",
        "ops-project": "admin"
      },
      "users": {}
    }
  }
}
```

- `remotes.<url>.active_users.<board-id>` = selected user for a concrete board on that remote
- `remotes.<url>.active_user` = remote-wide fallback before a board is pinned or when no board-specific selection exists
- `remotes.<url>.users` = legacy metadata container; in steady state tokens should be in Keychain, not here

Migration from legacy plaintext credentials:
```bash
task-board migrate-keychain --print-shell-cleanup
task-board migrate-keychain --cleanup-legacy-file
```

Other:
- sidecar default location: project directory (current working directory / `--project-dir` for setup scripts), not global/bin directory
- setup/install write unified config into project directory (default: current dir)
- CLI startup auto-bootstrap: if local board exists and config file does not exist, CLI writes default local profile (`mode=local`, `local.board_dir=.task-board`)

### Board Migration Workflows

Archive migration is now the canonical way to move a board between local and remote runtimes.

What is preserved:
- hierarchy (`epic -> story -> task/bug`)
- statuses
- assignee
- notes/checklists/dependencies
- precondition/outcome resource refs
- resource payload files

Core commands:
```bash
# Export current board to archive
task-board export /tmp/board.tar.gz

# Import archive into current board
task-board import /tmp/board.tar.gz
```

#### Local -> Remote

```bash
# source project (local mode)
cd ~/src/source-project
task-board export /tmp/source-board.tar.gz

# remote-connected project
cd ~/src/target-project
export TASK_BOARD_REMOTE=http://localhost:8080
export TASK_BOARD_USER=sampleuser
export TASK_BOARD_ID=my-project
task-board login sampleuser tb_xxxxx
task-board import /tmp/source-board.tar.gz
```

#### Remote -> Local

```bash
# remote-connected project
cd ~/src/source-remote-project
task-board export /tmp/remote-board.tar.gz

# target local project
cd ~/src/target-local-project
cat > task-board.config.json <<'JSON'
{
  "mode": "local",
  "local": {
    "board_dir": ".task-board"
  },
  "spawn": {
    "max_parallel": 20
  }
}
JSON

task-board import /tmp/remote-board.tar.gz
```

#### Round-trip Before Deploy

Use a locally deployed `board-server` to prove `local -> remote -> local` before touching the real remote service:

```bash
# 1. Build a fresh board-server binary for local service validation
cd ~/src/skill-project-management
make install-backend

# 2. Start local board-server
board-server --addr 127.0.0.1:8091 --data-dir /tmp/board-server-data/boards --auth-db /tmp/board-server-data/auth.db

# 3. Project-local remote config
cat > task-board.config.json <<'JSON'
{
  "mode": "remote",
  "spawn": {
    "max_parallel": 20
  },
  "remote": {
    "url": "http://127.0.0.1:8091",
    "board": "roundtrip-playground",
    "tls_no_verify": false
  }
}
JSON

# 4. Store token, create board, import archive
task-board login admin tb_xxxxx
task-board admin board-create roundtrip-playground "Roundtrip Playground"
task-board import /tmp/source-board.tar.gz

# 5. Export back out and import into a fresh local board
task-board export /tmp/roundtrip.tar.gz
```

For local-deployed service mode, if MinIO is not configured, `board-server` automatically uses a filesystem resource backend under `<dataDir>/.resources/`. That is the easiest way to validate resource payload round-trip during development.

